ARM64EC: native calls that keep the JIT's FPCR (a generated table of checked functions)

ExitFunctionEC writes FPCR on the way out of the JIT (to drop AH, NEP and the trap enables armed from MXCSR) and
FillSpecialRegs writes it back on the way in. On Apple cores a write to FPCR waits for everything in flight: about
8 ns each in a tight loop, more in real code. Microsoft Flight Simulator's main thread spent 8.3 % of its time on the
instruction after ExitFunctionEC's msr FPCR and 6 % after the dispatcher's (squark records 25.70, 25.77). 0036 skipped
both writes for memcpy, memmove and memset of Wine's builtin CRT; this extends it to every native function that
nothing those bits change can run in.

- FPCRNeutralTable.inc, generated when the runtime is built (squark-app runtime/fex/fpcrscan.py, run by
  build-wine.sh before FEX is compiled) from that build's ntdll, kernelbase, kernel32, ucrtbase and msvcrt: every
  function from which nothing reachable does floating-point data processing, writes FPCR or makes an indirect call the
  tool can't follow. It follows direct calls, jumps, tail calls, the import table (api sets and forwarders), switch
  tables, Wine's call checker for hybrid_patchable functions; calls into Wine's syscall and unix call dispatchers,
  ntdll's debug output entry points and its ARM64EC syscall wrappers end a path because Wine patch 0070 drops the
  JIT's bits there, and calls into FEX's own exports through ntdll's pointers because FEX drops them there too
  (NativeFPCR below). An image is used only when its name, TimeDateStamp and .text size are the scanned build's.
  Without the file the table is empty and only 0036's functions are listed.
- The targets live in an open-addressing set of 1 << 15 slots (linear probing, 0 ends a probe, 1 is a removed
  entry, at most 3/4 full) that ExitFunctionEC probes with eor/ubfx (no flags touched); 0036's linear list of 16 is
  gone. Images are added when mapped and their entries removed when unmapped, as before.
- FEX's exports that ntdll calls through pointers (Notify*, BTCpu64*, FlushInstructionCacheHeavy, GetX87State,
  Get/SetX64Mxcsr, KeepX64DebugRegisters, GuestProtection(Range), UpdateProcessorInformation) drop the JIT's FPCR
  bits first thing: such a call can now come from a function that runs with them. None of them reads or writes the
  live FPCR otherwise. The JIT writes the bits back when the call returns to x64 code.

SQUARK_FEX_FPCR_TABLE=0 leaves the generated table out (0036's memcpy, memmove and memset stay).

Measured with x64 calls in a loop under FEX (squark records 25.77), per call: GetLastError 26.3 -> 4.7 ns,
SetLastError 26.4 -> 4.7, GetCurrentThreadId 25.8 -> 4.0, TlsGetValue 26.2 -> 4.6, FlsGetValue 28.1 -> 6.9,
EnterCriticalSection + LeaveCriticalSection 59.8 -> 13.6, AcquireSRWLockShared + ReleaseSRWLockShared 70.8 -> 25.0;
cos (not listed) unchanged. With the simulator's exits of 25.77, the table covers 54 % of them, 72 % of the main
thread's.

